======================================================================
KEY INSIGHT
======================================================================
t-test family: 19 of 21 comparisons looked 'significant' before correction, and 16 (Bonferroni) / 19 (FDR) still do after correction — sample sizes per day (77k-93k users) are so large that even tiny, practically meaningless differences in mean ad exposure clear the corrected p-value bar. Every single one of those comparisons had a 'negligible' Cohen's d (|d| < 0.1, see ttest_comparisons output) — this is the textbook case of statistical significance without practical significance, and correction alone does not catch it. Effect size has to be checked too.
Fisher's exact family: 17 of 21 conversion-rate comparisons looked significant before correction; 17 survive Bonferroni and 17 survive FDR — almost unchanged, again because the sample sizes are large. Unlike the t-test family, several of these rate differences are also large in relative terms (10-35%), so they are more likely to be practically meaningful.
